---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SubNav from "../../components/SubNav.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import Card from "../../components/Card.astro";
---

<Base title="Crafting — Party Dungeon" current="/mechanics/crafting/">
  <SubNav current="/mechanics/crafting/" />

  <header>
    <div class="kicker"><a href="/mechanics/">Mechanics</a> / Crafting</div>
    <h1>You don't pick a recipe. You aim a weapon.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Each class crafts different things. Good gear is designed by hand in a
      crafting UI, then sold to somebody who can actually hold it.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section title="Three disciplines, held by classes">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="Smithing" tag="Class-locked">
        <p>
          Weapons and gear. The discipline with the most visible output, and the
          one the item designer below is written around.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Cooking" tag="Class-locked">
        <p>
          Food and the buffs that come with it. A party that eats better fights
          better, for a while.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Alchemy" tag="Class-locked">
        <p>
          Potions and reagents. The consumables you burn through in a run, made
          at home between them.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="An item is three layers">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="1 · Type" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Pick the kind of weapon to make. The type fixes the base and the
          <strong>materials</strong> it needs &mdash; a sword and a staff do not
          share a bill.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="2 · Runes" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Runes add the effects. Each one costs <strong>resources</strong>, so
          runes compete with each other for the same budget.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="3 · Requirements" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Every rune raises the <strong>stat bar</strong> for whoever wields the
          finished item &mdash; mana, strength, and the rest.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Level is the budget, design is the spend">
        <p>
          Levels set how much an item <em>can</em> be. The design decides where
          it goes. An NPC sword spends that budget evenly; a player smith can
          spend it on one purpose.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="The stat gate">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="No twinking">
        <p>
          A low-level player with endless materials still can't kit themselves
          out in endgame gear. The ceiling isn't gold or skill &mdash; it's their
          own stat line. Nobody gets carried by a gifted weapon.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="The smith's market points upward">
        <p>
          Great designs get sold to players who can hold them, so the best
          crafters serve the top of the level curve. The bottom buys NPC stock.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Requirements are the balance lever">
        <p>
          Same materials, same level, different rune mix: one sword is a
          strength weapon, another a mana weapon. That's how one discipline
          covers every class without a recipe per class.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Two runes, one wielder">
        <p>
          Runes that want the same stats can't coexist, even if the smith can
          pay for both. The wielder is a slot, and the design has to fit in it.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Finding a maker">
    <div class="split">
      <Card status="decided" title="A party is only half self-sufficient" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Class-gated crafting means your party will always be missing a
          discipline or two. When you want a new sword and nobody in the party
          is a smith, you <strong>find one or buy one</strong>.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Players and NPCs both sell" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          NPC vendors set a floor: competent, general, always available. Player
          crafters are the ones who can outclass them &mdash; with a design
          aimed at a purpose, not just a higher level.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Where it happens">
    <Card status="guess" title="A place, not a menu">
      <p>
        The forge, the kitchen, the lab &mdash; stations you build in your own
        dungeon. Which also means the place you craft in is the place visitors
        walk into.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </Section>
</Base>
